---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Full-screen Workshop 1 presentation" description="Visual, interactive presenter deck for Git, GitHub, VS Code, Copilot, and GitHub Pages.">
<div class="presentation-page container">
  <div class="presentation-intro no-print">
    <div><div class="eyebrow">MIS Club · Workshop 1 · presenter mode</div><h1>Build it. Version it. Ship it.</h1><p>This is the visual presentation workspace: diagrams, workflow demos, presenter notes, and quick-reference links are all inside the deck.</p></div>
    <button class="btn primary launch-fullscreen" data-fullscreen>Enter full screen ↗</button>
  </div>
  <div class="presentation-root" id="presentation-root">
    <header class="deck-topbar no-print">
      <a class="deck-brand" href={import.meta.env.BASE_URL}><span class="deck-mark">W</span><span>WICHITA STATE <small>MIS CLUB · WORKSHOP 1</small></span></a>
      <div class="deck-tools">
        <button class="deck-tool" data-toggle="resources" aria-haspopup="dialog">☷ Resources <kbd>R</kbd></button>
        <button class="deck-tool" data-toggle="notes">Presenter notes <kbd>N</kbd></button>
        <button class="deck-tool" data-fullscreen>⛶ Full screen <kbd>F</kbd></button>
      </div>
    </header>
    <div class="deck-stage" id="deck-stage" aria-live="polite">
      <section class="deck-slide work-slide cover-slide is-active" data-title="Git & GitHub: from folder to live site" data-notes="Set the promise for the session: students will run the site locally, make one focused change, record it in Git, push a branch, open a pull request, and verify the deployed result. Keep this slide as the overview, then move through the steps in order.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>MIS CLUB WORKSHOP 01 · FIELD GUIDE</span><span>3 HOURS · HANDS-ON</span></div>
        <div class="cover-work">
          <div class="cover-copy">
            <p class="eyebrow light">WICHITA STATE UNIVERSITY</p>
            <h2>Git &amp; GitHub:<br/><span>from folder to</span><br/>live site.</h2>
            <p class="cover-dek">A working guide for changing code, keeping a clean history, reviewing work, and publishing a real website.</p>
            <div class="cover-meta"><span>01 / WORKFLOW</span><span>02 / REVIEW</span><span>03 / DEPLOY</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="cover-index">
            <div class="index-line"><span>01</span><b>Run the project</b><small>Local first</small></div>
            <div class="index-line"><span>02</span><b>Make one small change</b><small>One clear outcome</small></div>
            <div class="index-line"><span>03</span><b>Commit and push</b><small>Keep the history readable</small></div>
            <div class="index-line"><span>04</span><b>Review and publish</b><small>Prove it works</small></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>KEEP THIS DECK OPEN WHILE YOU WORK</span><span>ARROWS TO MOVE · R FOR RESOURCES · N FOR NOTES</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="The target: one complete change" data-notes="Walk through the definition of done before students start. The target is not a perfect portfolio. The target is one focused, explainable change moving all the way from local code to a reviewed, deployed result. If students get stuck, they can use the checkpoint on the relevant slide rather than jumping ahead.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>START HERE</span><span>01 / PLAN</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">THE DEFINITION OF DONE</p><h2>One small change, all the way through.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">Do not try to build the whole portfolio today. Complete one useful improvement and practise the workflow.</p></div>
        <div class="target-grid">
          <div class="target-step"><span>01</span><h3>Run locally</h3><p>The project opens in VS Code and the local preview loads.</p></div>
          <div class="target-step"><span>02</span><h3>Change one thing</h3><p>Update one project, profile detail, or content section.</p></div>
          <div class="target-step"><span>03</span><h3>Record the work</h3><p>Check the diff, stage only intended files, and make a clear commit.</p></div>
          <div class="target-step"><span>04</span><h3>Review + publish</h3><p>Open a pull request, merge after review, then check the live URL.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="run-of-show">
          <div><b>20 min</b><span>Setup + project map</span></div><div><b>30 min</b><span>Git commands + commits</span></div><div><b>25 min</b><span>Branches + safe recovery</span></div><div><b>35 min</b><span>Issue, Copilot + review</span></div><div><b>10 min</b><span>Break + questions</span></div><div><b>30 min</b><span>Pull request + deployment</span></div><div><b>30 min</b><span>Guided lab + checkpoint</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>SUCCESS = A WORKING CHANGE YOU CAN EXPLAIN</span><span>DO THE STEPS IN ORDER</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="Step 1 — Open the project and run it" data-notes="Have students download the workshop starter or open their existing clone. They should be in the folder that contains package.json before they run npm commands. Leave this slide up until everyone sees the local site. If npm run dev fails, read the first error and check the folder before trying random fixes.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>STEP 1 · ENVIRONMENT</span><span>02 / SETUP</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">GET A KNOWN-GOOD START</p><h2>Open the project. Run it before editing.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">Start in the project root — the folder with <code>package.json</code>.</p></div>
        <div class="two-col">
          <div class="plain-panel">
            <h3>In VS Code</h3>
            <ol class="numbered-steps"><li>Download or clone the starter project.</li><li>In VS Code choose <b>File → Open Folder…</b></li><li>Select the project folder, not just one file.</li><li>Open <b>Terminal → New Terminal</b>.</li></ol>
            <div class="checkpoint"><b>Checkpoint</b><span>The Explorer shows <code>src/</code>, <code>public/</code>, and <code>package.json</code>.</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="cmd-panel">
            <div class="panel-label">TERMINAL · RUN IN PROJECT ROOT</div>
            <pre><code>npm install
npm run dev</code></pre>
            <div class="command-explainer"><code>npm install</code><span>Installs the packages listed by the project.</span></div>
            <div class="command-explainer"><code>npm run dev</code><span>Starts the local development server.</span></div>
            <div class="command-explainer"><code>Ctrl + C</code><span>Stops the server when you need the terminal back.</span></div>
            <div class="checkpoint"><b>Checkpoint</b><span>Open the local URL shown in the terminal, usually <code>http://localhost:4321</code>.</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>DON'T EDIT UNTIL THE STARTER RUNS</span><span>LOCAL PREVIEW FIRST</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="Know which file to change" data-notes="Point out that this starter keeps student-editable content separate from the page layout. Most content changes belong in src/data/profile.js. src/pages/index.astro defines the structure, and src/styles/global.css defines the visual design. package.json defines scripts and dependencies. The workflow file controls the automated deployment. Ask Copilot to locate the right file before asking it to edit anything.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>STEP 1 · PROJECT MAP</span><span>03 / FIND THE FILE</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">READ THE PROJECT BEFORE CHANGING IT</p><h2>Most tasks start with the right file.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">For this portfolio starter, change content before changing the page structure.</p></div>
        <div class="file-map">
          <div class="tree-panel"><div class="tree-root">portfolio-starter/</div><div class="tree-line"><span>▸</span><code>public/</code><small>Static assets</small></div><div class="tree-line active-file"><span>▾</span><code>src/data/profile.js</code><small>EDIT PROFILE + PROJECT CONTENT</small></div><div class="tree-line"><span>▸</span><code>src/pages/index.astro</code><small>Page layout + rendering</small></div><div class="tree-line"><span>▸</span><code>src/styles/global.css</code><small>Colors, spacing, responsive styles</small></div><div class="tree-line"><span>▸</span><code>astro.config.mjs</code><small>Build + base path</small></div><div class="tree-line"><span>▸</span><code>package.json</code><small>Commands + dependencies</small></div><div class="tree-line"><span>▸</span><code>.github/workflows/deploy.yml</code><small>Build + Pages deployment</small></div></div>
          <div class="file-guidance"><div class="file-callout"><span class="file-label">CONTENT TASK</span><h3>Start here</h3><code>src/data/profile.js</code><p>Update your bio, skills, education, experience, links, or projects using the existing shape.</p></div><div class="file-callout"><span class="file-label">LAYOUT TASK</span><h3>Only when needed</h3><code>src/pages/index.astro</code><p>Change how a section is displayed. Inspect the existing component before replacing it.</p></div><div class="file-callout"><span class="file-label">STYLE TASK</span><h3>Small, scoped changes</h3><code>src/styles/global.css</code><p>Use existing styles first. Check both a wide browser and a narrow mobile width.</p></div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>FIND THE SOURCE OF TRUTH BEFORE EDITING</span><span>ASK COPILOT TO EXPLAIN FIRST</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="The mental model — editor, Git, GitHub, Pages" data-notes="Keep this distinction practical. VS Code edits the local files. Git records local history. GitHub stores the remote repository and collaboration workflow. GitHub Actions builds the site. Pages serves the built output. A local commit does not automatically mean the remote or live website has changed.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>FOUNDATION</span><span>04 / THE MODEL</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">FIVE PARTS, DIFFERENT JOBS</p><h2>Know where the change is at each moment.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">The most common confusion is assuming a save, a commit, a push, and a deployment are the same thing.</p></div>
        <div class="pipeline">
          <div class="pipeline-node"><span>01</span><b>VS Code</b><p>Edit files on your computer.</p><small>SAVE</small></div><i>→</i><div class="pipeline-node"><span>02</span><b>Git</b><p>Stage and record a local snapshot.</p><small>COMMIT</small></div><i>→</i><div class="pipeline-node"><span>03</span><b>GitHub</b><p>Share commits and review the change.</p><small>PUSH / PR</small></div><i>→</i><div class="pipeline-node"><span>04</span><b>Actions</b><p>Install, build, and deploy.</p><small>WORKFLOW</small></div><i>→</i><div class="pipeline-node"><span>05</span><b>Pages</b><p>Serve the published site.</p><small>LIVE URL</small></div>
        </div>
        <div class="status-key"><div><b>Saved</b><span>The file on your computer changed.</span></div><div><b>Committed</b><span>Git recorded a snapshot in local history.</span></div><div><b>Pushed</b><span>Your commit was sent to GitHub.</span></div><div><b>Deployed</b><span>The automated publish finished; verify the URL.</span></div></div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>SAVE ≠ COMMIT ≠ PUSH ≠ DEPLOY</span><span>CHECK EACH STAGE</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="Git command quick reference" data-notes="This is the inspection toolkit. Have students run git status before doing anything else. Then inspect unstaged edits with git diff and the last few commits with git log. These commands are safe reads; they don't modify project files. If Git reports that this isn't a repository, they are probably in the wrong folder or need to initialize Git for a downloaded ZIP.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>STEP 2 · INSPECT BEFORE ACTING</span><span>05 / GIT BASICS</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">THE COMMANDS YOU'LL USE CONSTANTLY</p><h2>First: understand the current state.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">Run these from the project root. Start with <code>git status</code> when you're unsure.</p></div>
        <div class="command-reference">
          <div class="command-row"><code>git status</code><div><b>What is changed?</b><span>Shows the branch, staged files, unstaged edits, and untracked files.</span></div><em>SAFE · READ</em></div>
          <div class="command-row"><code>git diff</code><div><b>What did I edit?</b><span>Shows changes that are not staged yet.</span></div><em>SAFE · READ</em></div>
          <div class="command-row"><code>git diff --staged</code><div><b>What will I commit?</b><span>Shows the staged snapshot that the next commit will record.</span></div><em>SAFE · READ</em></div>
          <div class="command-row"><code>git log --oneline -5</code><div><b>What happened recently?</b><span>Shows the five latest commits in a compact list.</span></div><em>SAFE · READ</em></div>
          <div class="command-row"><code>git branch --show-current</code><div><b>Where am I working?</b><span>Prints the current branch name.</span></div><em>SAFE · READ</em></div>
          <div class="command-row"><code>git remote -v</code><div><b>Where does push go?</b><span>Shows the remote repository URL, normally named <code>origin</code>.</span></div><em>SAFE · READ</em></div>
        </div>
        <div class="bottom-note"><b>Rule:</b> read <code>git status</code> and the diff before you stage, commit, merge, or troubleshoot.</div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>INSPECT FIRST · MAKE ONE CHANGE AT A TIME</span><span>THESE COMMANDS DO NOT CHANGE FILES</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="The four-step commit cycle" data-notes="Demonstrate the distinction between add and commit. Stage one intentional file, inspect the staged diff, then create a commit with a useful verb and object. If there are unrelated changes in the working tree, do not blindly use git add .; stage the file or files relevant to this task.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>STEP 2 · RECORD A CHANGE</span><span>06 / COMMIT</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">THE CORE LOOP</p><h2>Inspect → stage → inspect → commit.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">A commit should describe one logical change. Review what you are about to save in history.</p></div>
        <div class="commit-flow">
          <div class="commit-stage"><span>01 · READ</span><pre><code>git status
git diff</code></pre><p>See what's changed. Confirm the file and the details.</p></div>
          <div class="commit-stage"><span>02 · STAGE</span><pre><code>git add src/data/profile.js</code></pre><p>Choose the file for this commit. Replace the path if your task changed another file.</p></div>
          <div class="commit-stage"><span>03 · REVIEW</span><pre><code>git diff --staged</code></pre><p>Confirm only the intended change is staged.</p></div>
          <div class="commit-stage"><span>04 · SAVE HISTORY</span><pre><code>git commit -m "Update project details"</code></pre><p>Record the staged snapshot with a clear message.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="commit-bottom"><div><b>Good commit</b><span><code>Add featured project</code></span><span><code>Fix mobile project spacing</code></span></div><div><b>Vague commit</b><span><code>updates</code></span><span><code>stuff</code></span></div><div class="checkpoint"><b>After committing</b><span>Run <code>git status</code> again; the working tree should be clean for this task.</span></div></div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>STAGING IS A CHOICE, NOT A FORMALITY</span><span>COMMIT WHAT YOU INTEND</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="First push — connect a local project to GitHub" data-notes="Use this path only if the project was downloaded as a ZIP and the student has created a new empty GitHub repository. The remote repository should not be pre-initialized with a README because the local starter already has project files and history. If the student cloned the repository instead, skip the git init/remote setup and use the existing origin.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>STEP 3 · SHARE WITH GITHUB</span><span>07 / REMOTE SETUP</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">ONE-TIME SETUP FOR A DOWNLOADED STARTER</p><h2>Connect local history to your repository.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">Create an empty repository on GitHub first. Then run these from the project folder.</p></div>
        <div class="remote-steps">
          <div class="remote-action"><span class="action-n">A</span><div><b>On GitHub</b><p>Select <b>New repository</b>. Give it a name. For this class, make it public only if you are comfortable publishing the content. Leave README, license, and .gitignore unchecked so the remote starts empty.</p></div></div>
          <div class="remote-action"><span class="action-n">B</span><div><b>Initialize and commit</b><pre><code>git init
git branch -M main
git add .
git diff --staged
git commit -m "Initial portfolio"</code></pre><p>Use <code>git add .</code> only after checking that the starter contains no secrets or personal files you do not intend to publish.</p></div></div>
          <div class="remote-action"><span class="action-n">C</span><div><b>Add the remote and push</b><pre><code>git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/YOUR-REPOSITORY.git
git remote -v
git push -u origin main</code></pre><p>Replace the example URL with the HTTPS URL for your own repository. The <code>-u</code> sets the upstream so future pushes can use <code>git push</code>.</p></div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="bottom-note warning-note"><b>If you cloned the repo:</b> do not run <code>git init</code> or add another remote. Run <code>git remote -v</code> to confirm <code>origin</code> already exists.</div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>ZIP DOWNLOAD = SET UP GIT + REMOTE</span><span>CLONE = REMOTE ALREADY EXISTS</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="Branch workflow — commands and purpose" data-notes="Explain that a branch keeps the feature separate from main. For each task, start from an up-to-date main, create a clearly named branch, commit a focused change, push that branch, and open a pull request on GitHub. If main has changed while the student worked, don't improvise a merge; inspect status and ask for help if conflict instructions are unclear.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>STEP 4 · SAFE, REVIEWABLE CHANGE</span><span>08 / BRANCHES</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">ONE TASK = ONE BRANCH</p><h2>Keep the change separate until it is reviewed.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">Use this sequence after the first push has succeeded.</p></div>
        <div class="branch-workflow">
          <div class="branch-commands"><div class="branch-command"><pre><code>git switch main
git pull</code></pre><p>Start from the latest main branch.</p></div><div class="branch-command"><pre><code>git switch -c feature/featured-project</code></pre><p>Create and move to a new branch.</p></div><div class="branch-command"><pre><code>git status
git diff</code></pre><p>Edit the project, then inspect the change.</p></div><div class="branch-command"><pre><code>git add src/data/profile.js
git diff --staged
git commit -m "Add featured project"</code></pre><p>Stage, review, and record one task.</p></div><div class="branch-command"><pre><code>git push -u origin feature/featured-project</code></pre><p>Publish the branch so GitHub can create a pull request.</p></div></div>
          <div class="branch-rules"><h3>Why these commands?</h3><div><code>git switch main</code><span>Move to the main branch.</span></div><div><code>git pull</code><span>Bring down remote changes before starting.</span></div><div><code>git switch -c NAME</code><span>Create a branch and switch to it.</span></div><div><code>git push -u origin NAME</code><span>Push the branch and set its upstream.</span></div><div class="checkpoint"><b>Check before editing</b><span><code>git branch --show-current</code> should print <code>feature/featured-project</code>.</span></div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>DON'T DO FEATURE WORK DIRECTLY ON MAIN</span><span>BRANCH → COMMIT → PUSH → PR</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="Recover safely — common undo commands" data-notes="Emphasize the difference between unstaged edits, staged changes, and committed work. The first two recovery commands shown here are scoped to one file and are useful for common mistakes. git restore FILE discards local edits to that file, so first make sure the edits are not needed. Avoid git reset --hard and force pushes in the workshop; ask for help rather than risking lost work.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>STEP 4 · RECOVERY</span><span>09 / UNDO SAFELY</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">UNDO IS NOT ONE THING</p><h2>Check the state before you try to undo it.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">Use the least destructive action that matches the state of your file.</p></div>
        <div class="recovery-table">
          <div class="recovery-head"><span>Situation</span><span>Command</span><span>What it does</span></div>
          <div class="recovery-row"><b>Unstage one file</b><code>git restore --staged src/data/profile.js</code><span>Removes it from the next commit; keeps your edits in the file.</span></div>
          <div class="recovery-row"><b>Discard edits to one file</b><code>git restore src/data/profile.js</code><span>Replaces that file with the last committed version. Your edits to it are lost.</span></div>
          <div class="recovery-row"><b>See the last commits</b><code>git log --oneline -5</code><span>Inspect history before choosing a recovery step.</span></div>
          <div class="recovery-row"><b>Confused or conflict shown</b><code>git status</code><span>Read the state. Stop and ask before running more commands.</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="safety-ribbon"><b>Do not use these as a guess:</b><code>git reset --hard</code><code>git push --force</code><span>They can discard work or overwrite shared history.</span></div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>NO PANIC COMMANDS</span><span>STATUS FIRST · ASK IF UNSURE</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="Create a useful issue before coding" data-notes="Use the issue to describe observable behavior. The example is intentionally small enough to complete in the workshop. Have students open Issues → New issue on their repository and adapt the title/criteria. The issue doesn't have to be elaborate; it must be specific enough that someone else can tell whether the task is done.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>STEP 5 · DEFINE THE TASK</span><span>10 / ISSUE</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">ISSUES MAKE THE WORK CHECKABLE</p><h2>Write down “done” before asking for code.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">Create an issue in GitHub: <b>Issues → New issue</b>. Use a clear title and acceptance criteria.</p></div>
        <div class="issue-work">
          <div class="issue-sheet"><div class="issue-top"><span class="issue-open">● Open</span><span>Issue template</span></div><h3>Add one featured project to the portfolio</h3><p><b>Goal</b> — Make one project easy for a recruiter or classmate to understand.</p><p><b>Acceptance criteria</b></p><ul class="criteria-list"><li>Project has an honest title and a short summary.</li><li>It names my role or contribution and relevant tools.</li><li>The project link works and opens the intended destination.</li><li>The layout remains readable on a narrow mobile screen.</li><li>No secrets or private information are added.</li></ul><div class="issue-labels"><span>enhancement</span><span>workshop</span></div></div>
          <div class="issue-instructions"><h3>On GitHub</h3><ol class="numbered-steps"><li>Open your repository.</li><li>Select <b>Issues → New issue</b>.</li><li>Copy the title and adapt the criteria.</li><li>Create the issue before making the code change.</li></ol><div class="checkpoint"><b>Checkpoint</b><span>Could someone who didn't write the code verify each criterion? If not, make it more specific.</span></div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>REQUIREMENTS FIRST</span><span>REVIEW THE RESULT AGAINST THE CRITERIA</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="Use Copilot with a reviewable prompt" data-notes="Have students use a two-pass workflow: ask it to locate and explain relevant code without edits, then request one constrained change. A good prompt names the file, desired behavior, existing conventions, non-goals, and how to validate the result. Students must use accurate content, particularly for biography, experience, and project outcomes.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>STEP 6 · AI-ASSISTED EDITING</span><span>11 / COPILOT</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">CONTEXT + GOAL + CONSTRAINTS + VALIDATION</p><h2>Ask for a small, explainable change.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">Use Copilot Chat in VS Code. Don't ask it to “make the whole site better.”</p></div>
        <div class="prompt-workflow">
          <div class="prompt-card"><div class="prompt-head"><span>PASS 1</span><b>Understand first — no edits</b></div><pre><code>Inspect this Astro portfolio project. Do not edit any files yet.
Tell me which file contains the profile and project content,
how the projects are rendered, and which command builds the site.
Keep the explanation beginner-friendly and name the exact files.</code></pre><p>Read the answer. Open the files it names and confirm the explanation.</p></div>
          <div class="prompt-card"><div class="prompt-head"><span>PASS 2</span><b>Request one focused edit</b></div><pre><code>In src/data/profile.js, update the project content for this task:
[describe my real project and my contribution].
Keep the existing data structure and visual design.
Do not add dependencies or edit other files.
If the current structure is unclear, ask before changing it.
After the edit, explain every change and how I can verify it.</code></pre><p>Replace the bracketed instruction with truthful content. Review each file changed.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="ai-rules"><div><b>Never paste</b><span>Passwords, API keys, private keys, tokens, or sensitive personal data.</span></div><div><b>Never assume</b><span>Generated code, claims, links, and accessibility are correct.</span></div><div><b>Keep ownership</b><span>You must be able to explain and maintain the change you submit.</span></div></div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>AI PROPOSES · YOU REVIEW · YOU OWN THE RESULT</span><span>ONE TASK PER PROMPT</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="Review the diff and run the build" data-notes="This is the quality gate before committing or opening a PR. Students should understand the diff, run the local preview, build the site, and check that they didn't accidentally include environment files or secrets. If the build fails, read the first relevant error instead of asking Copilot to rewrite the entire project.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>STEP 7 · VERIFY BEFORE REVIEW</span><span>12 / QUALITY CHECK</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">REVIEW YOUR OWN WORK FIRST</p><h2>Don't submit code you haven't inspected.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">Use the same checks every time, even when the code was written with AI assistance.</p></div>
        <div class="verify-grid">
          <div class="verify-terminal"><div class="panel-label">TERMINAL · PROJECT ROOT</div><pre><code>git status
git diff --check
git diff
npm run build</code></pre><div class="verify-result"><b>Expected result</b><span>Build completes without an error. Diff contains only the intended change.</span></div></div>
          <div class="review-checklist"><h3>Human review checklist</h3><label><i>□</i><span>Every changed file is needed for this task.</span></label><label><i>□</i><span>I can explain the changes line by line at a high level.</span></label><label><i>□</i><span>No invented facts, credentials, tokens, or private data.</span></label><label><i>□</i><span>Links, headings, keyboard focus, and mobile layout work.</span></label><label><i>□</i><span>Acceptance criteria are met in the local browser.</span></label><label><i>□</i><span>The workflow or configuration wasn't changed unnecessarily.</span></label><div class="checkpoint"><b>If a check fails</b><span>Fix one cause, rerun the failed check, and inspect the new diff.</span></div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>LOCAL TESTS BEFORE GIT PUSH</span><span>FIX THE SMALLEST CAUSE</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="Open and review a pull request" data-notes="Now switch to GitHub in the browser. After pushing the feature branch, use the banner or Pull requests → New pull request. Confirm base is main and compare is the feature branch. In the PR, summarize what changed, link the issue, explain how it was tested, and inspect the Files changed tab. Merge only when the diff and acceptance criteria are satisfactory.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>STEP 8 · REVIEW ON GITHUB</span><span>13 / PULL REQUEST</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">A PULL REQUEST IS A REVIEW BOUNDARY</p><h2>Compare the change before it joins main.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">After <code>git push -u origin feature/featured-project</code>, open GitHub in your browser.</p></div>
        <div class="pr-workflow">
          <div class="pr-steps"><div><span>1</span><p>Open the repo and select <b>Compare &amp; pull request</b>, or go to <b>Pull requests → New pull request</b>.</p></div><div><span>2</span><p>Set <b>base: main</b> and <b>compare: feature/featured-project</b>.</p></div><div><span>3</span><p>Enter a clear title and summarize the change, tests, and any limitations.</p></div><div><span>4</span><p>Open <b>Files changed</b>. Read the diff and check the issue criteria.</p></div><div><span>5</span><p>Merge only when the change is correct. Confirm the branch update in the repository.</p></div></div>
          <div class="pr-template"><div class="pr-template-head"><span class="pr-green">✓ Open</span><b>Add featured project</b><small>feature/featured-project → main</small></div><div class="pr-template-body"><b>What changed?</b><p>Added one featured project using the existing profile data structure.</p><b>How was it tested?</b><p><code>npm run build</code>; checked desktop + mobile preview; verified project link.</p><b>Acceptance criteria</b><p>✓ Summary · ✓ Contribution · ✓ Link · ✓ Responsive · ✓ No secrets</p><div class="pr-diff"><span>src/data/profile.js</span><span class="diff-plus">+ added project details</span></div></div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="bottom-note"><b>PR rule:</b> describe what changed, how you checked it, and any risks. “Copilot wrote it” is not a review summary.</div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>BRANCH → PULL REQUEST → REVIEW → MERGE</span><span>CHECK THE FILES CHANGED TAB</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="Publish with GitHub Pages and Actions" data-notes="Explain how this workshop starter is deployed: pushing to main triggers GitHub Actions, which installs dependencies and runs npm run build, uploads dist, and deploys to Pages. A successful local build does not automatically publish. A feature branch does not publish to the live site until its PR is merged to main.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>STEP 9 · DEPLOYMENT</span><span>14 / PAGES + ACTIONS</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">PUBLISH THE BUILT FILES, NOT THE SOURCE FOLDER</p><h2>Merge to main. Watch the workflow. Test the URL.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">For this starter, GitHub Actions is the build and deployment path.</p></div>
        <div class="deploy-workflow">
          <div class="deploy-step"><span>01</span><b>Push / merge</b><p>New commit reaches <code>main</code>.</p></div><i>→</i><div class="deploy-step dark-step"><span>02</span><b>Actions runs</b><p><code>npm install</code><br/><code>npm run build</code><br/>Uploads <code>dist/</code></p></div><i>→</i><div class="deploy-step"><span>03</span><b>Pages deploys</b><p>Static output is published to the site URL.</p></div><i>→</i><div class="deploy-step"><span>04</span><b>Verify live</b><p>Open the URL and check content, links, and assets.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="pages-settings">
          <div><b>To check configuration</b><ol><li>Open the GitHub repository.</li><li>Select <b>Settings → Pages</b>.</li><li>Confirm source is <b>GitHub Actions</b>.</li></ol></div>
          <div><b>To check a deploy</b><ol><li>Open the <b>Actions</b> tab.</li><li>Open the latest run for your commit.</li><li>Read the failed step or confirm green success.</li><li>Open the deployed URL from the run or repository.</li></ol></div>
        </div>
        <div class="bottom-note warning-note"><b>Important:</b> pushing a feature branch is not the same as publishing. For this workshop, merge the reviewed PR into <code>main</code>, then wait for Actions.</div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>MAIN → ACTIONS → PAGES</span><span>A GREEN RUN IS NOT ENOUGH: TEST THE REAL URL</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="Troubleshooting — start with the evidence" data-notes="Have students classify the symptom before editing anything. On Actions failure, open the failed job and find the first meaningful error. For 404s, inspect the exact deployed URL and Pages settings. For assets, check base paths. For push problems, start with status and remote; do not force push. Work one hypothesis at a time.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>STEP 10 · DEBUG</span><span>15 / TROUBLESHOOTING</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">SYMPTOM → FIRST CHECK → NEXT ACTION</p><h2>Don't guess. Narrow the failure.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">Start with the symptom, inspect evidence, and change one thing at a time.</p></div>
        <div class="troubleshooting">
          <div class="trouble-row"><b>“not a git repository”</b><span>Run <code>pwd</code> / inspect the folder. Move to the project root that contains <code>package.json</code>; use <code>git init</code> only for a downloaded ZIP without Git.</span></div>
          <div class="trouble-row"><b>Push rejected</b><span>Run <code>git status</code> and <code>git remote -v</code>. Stop and inspect why the remote differs; don't force push or blindly overwrite history.</span></div>
          <div class="trouble-row"><b>Build failed in Actions</b><span>Open the failed job log and find the first actionable error. Reproduce locally with <code>npm run build</code>; fix the referenced file and push again.</span></div>
          <div class="trouble-row"><b>Site URL returns 404</b><span>Check Settings → Pages, confirm the latest workflow succeeded, and use the full project URL including the repository path.</span></div>
          <div class="trouble-row"><b>HTML loads, styles/assets don't</b><span>Check the asset URL in browser dev tools and the Astro base path. A leading slash can point at the domain root rather than the project subpath.</span></div>
          <div class="trouble-row"><b>Latest change isn't visible</b><span>Confirm the PR was merged to <code>main</code>, the newest Actions run succeeded, the URL is correct, then hard refresh.</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="bottom-note"><b>Useful diagnostic order:</b> <code>git status</code> → reproduce locally → read the first actionable error → smallest fix → rerun the check.</div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>THE FIRST ACTIONABLE ERROR IS YOUR CLUE</span><span>DON'T CHANGE CONFIGURATION AT RANDOM</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="Hands-on lab — implement the issue" data-notes="Give students 30 minutes here. Have them begin on the feature branch, then change their project data and follow the review checklist. Walk around and ask students to explain the task, the relevant file, and the diff before they ask you to debug. A complete small task is better than an ambitious unfinished redesign.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>LAB · BUILD</span><span>16 / WORK ALONG</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">YOUR TURN · 30 MINUTES</p><h2>Implement the featured project issue.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">Keep this slide up while you work. Complete the steps in order; don't skip the review.</p></div>
        <div class="lab-layout">
          <div class="lab-checklist"><div class="lab-title">WORK CHECKLIST</div><label><i>□</i><span>Issue exists with testable acceptance criteria.</span></label><label><i>□</i><span>Working branch is <code>feature/featured-project</code>.</span></label><label><i>□</i><span>One real project is added in <code>src/data/profile.js</code>.</span></label><label><i>□</i><span>Local preview shows the project and its link works.</span></label><label><i>□</i><span><code>git diff</code> shows only the intended files.</span></label><label><i>□</i><span><code>npm run build</code> completes.</span></label><label><i>□</i><span>Commit message explains the change.</span></label><label><i>□</i><span>Feature branch is pushed; PR is open.</span></label></div>
          <div class="lab-steps"><div><span>1</span><b>Create the branch</b><code>git switch main</code><code>git pull</code><code>git switch -c feature/featured-project</code></div><div><span>2</span><b>Edit + inspect</b><p>Update the project data. Run <code>npm run dev</code>, check desktop and mobile, then inspect <code>git diff</code>.</p></div><div><span>3</span><b>Commit + push</b><code>git add src/data/profile.js</code><code>git diff --staged</code><code>git commit -m "Add featured project"</code><code>git push -u origin feature/featured-project</code></div><div><span>4</span><b>Open the PR</b><p>Link or reference the issue, list checks performed, review Files changed, then merge when ready.</p></div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>ASK FOR HELP WITH THE ERROR, NOT JUST “IT DOESN'T WORK”</span><span>SHOW YOUR STATUS + DIFF</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="Lab checkpoint — explain the change" data-notes="Use the checkpoint to make students explain the result rather than just finish the commands. Ask them to show the issue, branch, changed file, commit, PR, and live URL. If deployment hasn't finished, they can still demonstrate the successful local build and Actions status.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>LAB · REVIEW</span><span>17 / CHECKPOINT</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">SHOW YOUR WORK</p><h2>Be able to answer these without guessing.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">The workflow is complete when you can show evidence at each stage.</p></div>
        <div class="checkpoint-grid">
          <div><span>01</span><h3>Task</h3><p>Which issue did you implement? How does the result meet each criterion?</p><b>Show the issue.</b></div>
          <div><span>02</span><h3>Code</h3><p>Which file changed? Why was it the right file? What does the diff show?</p><b>Show the file + diff.</b></div>
          <div><span>03</span><h3>History</h3><p>What branch and commit hold the change? Why is the message useful?</p><b>Show <code>git log</code>.</b></div>
          <div><span>04</span><h3>Review</h3><p>What did you test? What review feedback or acceptance check matters most?</p><b>Show the PR.</b></div>
          <div><span>05</span><h3>Deployment</h3><p>Did Actions succeed? Have you checked the actual published page, not just local preview?</p><b>Show Actions + URL.</b></div>
        </div>
        <div class="checkpoint"><b>Instructor check</b><span>Ask the student to explain one line changed by Copilot and one check they used to verify it.</span></div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>EXPLAIN THE CHANGE, NOT JUST THE TOOL</span><span>WORKING · REVIEWABLE · PUBLISHED</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide work-slide" data-title="Keep-this-open Git cheat sheet" data-notes="Leave this final reference slide up for the lab or Q&A. Remind students that Git status is the first diagnostic command, git diff shows what changed, staged diff shows what will be committed, and the push/PR workflow is branch based. Point them to Resources for the custom walkthrough and starter archive.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>QUICK REFERENCE</span><span>18 / CHEAT SHEET</span></div>
        <div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">WHEN YOU FORGET THE NEXT COMMAND</p><h2>Start here. Read the output.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">This is the compact sequence to leave on screen during independent work.</p></div>
        <div class="cheat-columns">
          <div class="cheat-section"><h3>Inspect</h3><pre><code>git status
git diff
git diff --staged
git log --oneline -5
git branch --show-current</code></pre></div>
          <div class="cheat-section"><h3>Record</h3><pre><code>git add path/to/file
git diff --staged
git commit -m "Describe the change"
git status</code></pre></div>
          <div class="cheat-section"><h3>Branch + share</h3><pre><code>git switch main
git pull
git switch -c feature/name
git push -u origin feature/name</code></pre></div>
          <div class="cheat-section"><h3>Validate + publish</h3><pre><code>npm run dev
npm run build
# push branch → open PR
# merge → check Actions
# open and test live URL</code></pre></div>
        </div>
        <div class="exit-ticket"><b>EXIT TICKET</b><span>What changed? Where is the diff? What proves it works? What happens after merge?</span></div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>STATUS FIRST · DIFF ALWAYS · VERIFY THE LIVE URL</span><span>RESOURCES: PRESS R</span></div>
      </section>
    </div>
